TITLE:: RCSieve
summary:: A sieve (Xenakis): a set of integers built from residue classes, for scales and time grids
categories:: Libraries>reCurrent
related:: Classes/RCLaws, Classes/RCMarkov, Classes/RCRhythm, Classes/RCLines

DESCRIPTION::
Xenakis' sieve theory (emphasis::Formalized Music::, ch. XI-XII) builds scales, rhythms and any well-ordered set from residue classes. A pair code::(M, I):: is the class of the integers congruent to I modulo M; a formula unites intersections of pairs:
code::
[[[3, 2], [4, 7]], [[6, 9], [15, 18]]]      // (3,2)∩(4,7) ∪ (6,9)∩(15,18)
::
A sieve is a formula with a strong::unit:: and a strong::zero::: the integer n sounds at code::zero + n * unit:: (pitch in octaves: unit 1/12 a semitone, 1/24 a quartertone; time in beats: unit 1/4 a sixteenth). Membership is tested arithmetically; an intersection of pairs reduces to one pair (the lcm and the first coincidence, p. 271-273), an empty one drops out. A sieve is a value: equality and hash are structural.

A strong::tuned:: sieve counts its integers as the steps of a tuning instead of equal units: the formula picks the steps, the tuning says where each one sounds. With S steps per period, n sounds at code::zero + (n div S) * period + steps[n mod S]:: (in the sieve's units: octaves for a pitch sieve); a fixed field (a tuning that does not repeat) sounds n at code::zero + steps[n]:: for code::0 <= n < S::. The unit is then unused. A tuning is a link::Classes/Tuning:: (its semitones / 12, its period code::octaveRatio.log2::), a link::Classes/Scale:: (its degrees become the steps), a Symbol naming a Tuning or else a Scale, or an Array of values (a fixed field). A periodic tuning of your own is a code::Tuning(semitones, octaveRatio)::; link::#*readScala:: reads a Scala file into one.

code::
~diatonic = RCSieve.residues(12, [0, 2, 4, 5, 7, 9, 11], 1/12, 0, \diatonic);   // the white keys (p. 269)
~diatonic.points(0, 0.99);                 // [0, 1/6, 1/3, 5/12, 7/12, 3/4, 11/12]: the degrees of one octave
~diatonic.metabola(7).points(0, 0.99);     // the same structure a fifth up (p. 275-276)
~book = RCSieve([[[3, 2], [4, 7]], [[6, 9], [15, 18]]], 1/24);
~book.pairs;                               // [[12, 11], [30, 3]] (p. 278), period 60
~diatonic.tuned(\just).points(0, 0.99);   // the same seven degrees in just intonation: E at log2(5/4)
RCSieve.residues(19, (0..18), 1, 0, \et19, Tuning.et(19)).points(0, 0.99).size;   // 19
RCSieve.residues(2, [0], 1, 0, \odd, (1..32).log2).points(0, 5);   // the odd partials up to 31, a fixed field
RCSieve.fromPoints([0, 3, 6, 8, 11, 14, 16, 19, 22]).pairs;   // [[8, 0], [8, 3], [8, 6]]: the 3, 3, 2 rhythm read back
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: formula
An Array of unions, each an Array of code::[modulus, residue]:: pairs intersected. Residues are reduced modulo their modulus.
argument:: unit
The value of one integer step (1 by default).
argument:: zero
The value of the integer 0.
argument:: name
argument:: tuning
nil (equal units), a Tuning, a Scale, a Symbol naming one, or an Array of values (a fixed field).

METHOD:: residues
The union of the residues of one modulus: code::residues(8, [0, 3, 6], 1/2):: is the 3, 3, 2 rhythm in eighths. Takes a strong::tuning:: last, as code::new::.

METHOD:: tuningSteps
A tuning's steps (sorted, in octaves) and its period in octaves (nil: a fixed field), as code::[steps, period]::, or nil for no tuning.

METHOD:: readScala
A link::Classes/Tuning:: from a Scala code::.scl:: file, or from its text: the description, the count, then a pitch per line (cents when it has a decimal point, else a ratio code::a/b:: or an integer), code::1/1:: implicit and the last pitch the period. Lines starting with code::!:: are comments. nil, warned, when it cannot be read.
code::
RCSieve.readScala("~/scales/slendro.scl");
::

METHOD:: residuesFormula
That union as a formula.

METHOD:: fromPoints
METHOD:: formulaFromPoints
The inverse (p. 274-275, simplified): from a set of integers, the union of the code::(Q, I):: pairs whose every point within the set's span belongs to the set, the smallest modulus first, each point covered once; a point no modulus up to strong::maxModulus:: explains gets the span as its modulus. The result reproduces the set over its span.

METHOD:: intersect
code::(M1, I1) ∩ (M2, I2):: as code::[M3, I3]:: (M3 the lcm, I3 the first coincidence), or nil when the classes never meet.
code::
RCSieve.intersect([3, 2], [4, 7]);   // [12, 11]
RCSieve.intersect([6, 3], [8, 4]);   // nil
::

METHOD:: simplify
A formula with every union reduced to one pair.

METHOD:: periodOf
The period of a formula: the lcm of its unions' moduli (p. 275: code::(24,23) ∪ (30,3) ∪ (104,70):: → 1560).

METHOD:: formulaIncludes
METHOD:: formulaIntegers
Membership of an integer, and the integers of a formula in a range, without a sieve object.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: formula
METHOD:: unit
METHOD:: zero
METHOD:: name

METHOD:: includes
Whether the integer n belongs to the sieve.

METHOD:: integers
The integers of the sieve in [lo, hi].

METHOD:: points
The values of the sieve within [lo, hi], unit and zero applied (or its tuning), ascending.

METHOD:: pointAt
The value the integer n sounds at; nil outside a fixed field.

METHOD:: isTuned
METHOD:: tuning
METHOD:: tuningSteps
METHOD:: tuningPeriod
The tuning as given, its steps in octaves, its period in octaves (nil for a fixed field).

METHOD:: tuned
The same structure in another tuning (nil: back to equal units), a new sieve: the third metabola for tunings.

METHOD:: pairs
The simplified pairs, one per union.

METHOD:: simplified
A new sieve with the simplified formula.

METHOD:: period

METHOD:: metabola
A new sieve: strong::shift:: a number adds it to every residue (a transposition), an Array gives one shift per pair in order (the intervallic structure changes, the period stays; p. 276: +3, +1, -6 on code::(5,4) ∪ (3,2) ∪ (7,3):: gives code::(5,2) ∪ (3,0) ∪ (7,4)::); strong::unit:: replaces the unit (the diatonic structure in quartertones). The tuning is kept: on a tuned sieve a transposition moves by steps of the tuning, and its intervals change as modes do.

EXAMPLES::
code::
// a pitch field for a texture cell: the points of the sieve inside a band, in octaves over 240 Hz
~field = RCSieve([[[3, 0]], [[4, 0]]], 1/12);     // 3_0 ∪ 4_0: C D# E F# G# A (p. 270)
~field.points(-1.5, 1.5);

// a time grid for a cycle of 4 beats: Analogique's 5 + 4 + 3 subdivisions
~grid = RCSieve.residues(12, [0], 4/60) ; // one modulus; the union of three:
~grid = RCSieve(RCSieve.residuesFormula(12, [0]) ++ RCSieve.residuesFormula(15, [0]) ++ RCSieve.residuesFormula(20, [0]), 4/60);
~grid.points(0, 4 - 1e-6).size;   // 10 distinct positions
::
